INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'us-jobs-report-october-2026-payrolls-165000-20261106', 'global', 'United States',
'US Jobs Report October 2026: Payrolls Jump 165,000 as Unemployment Falls to 4.1%',
'',
'The US jobs report for October 2026 beat forecasts by a wide margin, with employers adding 165,000 jobs and unemployment falling to 4.1%. Professional and business services led the hiring, while revisions added 84,000 jobs to the previous two months.',
'',
$$<p>The US jobs report October 2026 beat expectations by a wide margin, the Bureau of Labor Statistics said on 6 November 2026. Employers added 165,000 jobs during the month, more than double the 65,000 economists polled by Reuters had forecast, and the unemployment rate fell to 4.1% from 4.2% in September. The bureau also revised its figures for the previous two months higher, adding a combined 84,000 jobs to earlier estimates, in a report that landed a day after Wall Street suffered its worst sell-off since April 2025.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>US jobs report, October 2026</th></tr><tr><td>Report</td><td>Employment Situation, October 2026, Bureau of Labor Statistics, released 6 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Nonfarm payrolls</td><td>Up 165,000; economists polled by Reuters had forecast 65,000</td></tr><tr><td>Unemployment rate</td><td>4.1%, down from 4.2% in September</td></tr><tr><td>August revision</td><td>Revised up by 53,000, from 133,000 to 186,000</td></tr><tr><td>September revision</td><td>Revised up by 31,000, from 29,000 to 60,000</td></tr><tr><td>Average monthly gain, prior 12 months</td><td>48,000</td></tr><tr><td>Professional and business services</td><td>Up 70,000, against an average monthly gain of 15,000 over the prior 12 months</td></tr><tr><td>Average hourly earnings</td><td>$38.00, up 14 cents or 0.4% on the month, and up 3.1% over the past 12 months</td></tr><tr><td>Labour force participation rate</td><td>61.9%</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term unemployed</td><td>2.0 million, or 27.6% of all unemployed people</td></tr><tr><td>Next data check</td><td>Consumer inflation report for October, due 10 November 2026</td></tr></table>
<h2>What the US jobs report October 2026 showed</h2>
<p>Employment rose in professional and business services, health care, and transportation and warehousing, the bureau said. Professional and business services added 70,000 jobs, far above its average monthly gain of 15,000 over the previous 12 months, and the sector has added 269,000 jobs since a recent low in October 2025. The headline gain of 165,000 was more than three times the average monthly increase of 48,000 over the previous year, a sign that hiring picked up speed after a soft September rather than drifting lower.</p>
<p>Revisions told the same stronger story. August was revised up by 53,000 to 186,000, and September was revised up by 31,000 to 60,000. Average hourly earnings rose by 14 cents, or 0.4%, to $38.00 in October, and pay is up 3.1% over the past 12 months, slower than the 3.2% recorded in September. The labour force participation rate held at 61.9%. Beneath the headline, the bureau reported that the number of people working part time for economic reasons rose by 339,000 to 4.8 million, and long-term unemployment held at 2.0 million, or 27.6% of all unemployed people.</p>
<p>Not every sector shared in the gains. Technology hiring remains under pressure even as the wider market strengthens: <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/servicenow-layoffs-ai-restructuring-us-uk-india-20261106">ServiceNow cut jobs in the US, UK and India in an AI restructuring</a> this week, and Block announced a second round of layoffs. The contrast shows a labour market splitting in two, with business services, health care and warehousing adding staff while parts of the technology sector still trim roles.</p>
<h2>Why employers are hiring cautiously despite the strong headline</h2>
<p>The strong headline sits alongside a cautious mood among employers. Reuters reported that layoffs remain historically low and job openings have jumped, raising the prospect of a tighter labour market. But economists said many employers overhired after the Covid pandemic and are still unsure how many staff they need as they bring artificial intelligence into their operations. The Bank of England found the same pattern in Britain this week, where <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-jobs-uk-bank-of-england-hiring-freeze-20261106">AI is freezing hiring rather than causing redundancies</a>, with firms leaving vacancies unfilled instead of dismissing workers.</p>
<p>Other forces are also shaping the numbers. Reuters reported that a shrinking supply of workers, as immigration tightens and older workers retire, is helping to hold unemployment down, and economists estimated the economy now needs only about 40,000 new jobs a month to keep up with growth in the working-age population, down from roughly 150,000 in 2024. Uncertainty over the US-Israel war with Iran, now in its ninth month, has pushed energy prices higher and made some employers hesitant to add headcount. Gross domestic product grew at a 3.4% annualised rate in the third quarter, fuelled by strong consumer spending and business investment in artificial intelligence, yet job gains are still lagging that growth.</p>
<p>The report also changes the outlook for interest rates. The Federal Reserve left its benchmark overnight rate in the 3.75% to 4.00% range last week, and Reuters reported that economists expect a rate rise in December after the strong jobs figures. The next test comes on 10 November 2026, when the consumer inflation report for October is published, and that reading could shape the course of monetary policy in early 2027.</p>
<h2>What to do next if you are looking for work in the US</h2>
<p>Jobseekers should aim where the hiring is. Professional and business services, health care, and transportation and warehousing added jobs in October, and those sectors are the most direct place to look first. Young adults and recent graduates face the hardest market: Reuters reported that limited opportunities have made it difficult for young adults, recent college graduates and some unemployed people to find work, so new entrants should widen their search beyond the most crowded graduate roles and consider the sectors that are actually adding staff.</p>
<p>Apply through an employer's own careers page or a recognised job board, and never pay anyone for a job offer, an interview, or a visa. Genuine US employers do not charge applicants, and any message asking for a fee, a gift card, or a payment to secure a role is a scam. Keep a record of every application, and judge the market by the trend rather than a single month: one strong report does not settle the direction of hiring, and the December rate decision will shape recruitment into 2027.</p>
<p>Canada told a different story on the same day, with unemployment rising as its economy lost jobs. Read the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/canada-jobs-report-october-2026-unemployment-67-20261106">Canada jobs report October 2026</a> to see how the two North American labour markets are moving in opposite directions.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Bureau of Labor Statistics Employment Situation release for October 2026, as reported by Reuters at https://www.reuters.com/business/us-job-growth-beats-expectations-october-unemployment-rate-falls-41-2026-11-06/ on the headline figures, the revisions, the Federal Reserve outlook and the outlook for young workers; JBizNews at https://jbiznews.com/october-jobs-blow-past-expectations-u-s-adds-165000-unemployment-slips-to-4-1/ on wages, part-time work and long-term unemployment, published 6 November 2026.</small></p>$$,
